Artificial nanozyme based on platinum nanoparticles anchored metal-organic frameworks with enhanced electrocatalytic activity for detection of telomeres activity
Laboratory of Functionalized Molecular Solids, Ministry of Education, Anhui Key Laboratory of Chemo/Biosensing, College of Chemistry and Materials Science, Anhui Normal University, Wuhu 241002, P. R. China.
Abstract
This study reports a new artificial nanozyme based on ultra-small Pt nanoparticles (Pt NPs) grown on nanoscale metalloporphyrin metal organic frameworks (P-MOF(Fe)) (termed as Pt@P-MOF(Fe)) as biomimetic catalysts and redox mediator to detect the telomerase activity. In this system, the P-MOF(Fe) were used as nanocarrier and signal media. The DNA functionalized Pt@P-MOF(Fe) was as signal probe and exhibited enhanced electrochemical signal in the presence of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), owing to the synergistic effect between P-MOF(Fe) and Pt NPs. Upon the addition cell extract, the telomerase primer could extend and then hybridize with assistant DNA2 in the triple-helix, leading to the structure of triple-helix changes and release the hairpin DNA to hybridize with the capture DNA on the surface of Pt@P-MOF(Fe), resulting in the electrochemical signal readout of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) reduction. With the aid of recycling amplification of Exonuclease III, the telomeres sensor exhibited the detection down to 20 Hela cell \(\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\). This work supplies a new avenue to design artificial enzyme catalysts and serves as an ideal platform to use metalloporphyrin metal organic frameworks as signal media for detection of analytes. Keywords: Electrochemical sensor; Artificial nanozymes; Metal-organic framework; Telomeres; Signal amplification
## 1. Introduction
Telomeres are caps with repetitive nucleotide sequences ((TTAGGG)n) at the termini of each DNA of most eukaryotic organisms to protect the chromosomes from untimely degradation (Blackburn et al., 2012; Meyne et al., 1989). It reported that telomerase can be reactivated and reset back to an embryonic state, so it has a role in senescence, cell growth and the prevention of cancer (lanza et al., 2000; Shay et al., 2005; Rodier et al., 2011). Usually, the telomerase activity is overexpressed in almost all cancer cells, therefore, it could be acted as a specific tumor marker to early clinical diagnostics and understand the disease mechanisms. Up to now, various methods have been designed for detection of telomerase activity, including PCR-based telomeric repeat amplification protocols (Herbert et al., 2006; Kim et al., 1997; Hou et al., 2001; Cawthon et al., 2002), electrochemiluminescence (Sharon et al., 2014), fluorescence (Yang et al., 2017; Liu et al., 2018) as well as Raman (Ma et al., 2017; Xu et al., 2016). These strategies have achieved satisfactory sensitivity and provided platform for detection of telomerase activity. However, most of these methods still require complicated manipulation and time cost. As we all know, electrochemical methods are simple, highly sensitive, portable and miniaturization. They could be used in and on-field application and have been utilized in environmental analysis (Beitollahi et al., 2015) and biological analysis (Beitollahi et al., 2018, 2013; Tajik et al., 2013). For example, the telomerase electrochemical biosensor was designed with porphyrinic metal–organic framework or platinum nanoparticles encapsulated metal–organic frameworks for the telomerase activity detection (Ling et al., 2016). An electrochemical biosensor based on structure-switching DNA probe and ferrocene as the electroactive reporter was used to detect telomerase activity (Zi et al., 2014). In the above approaches, the signal probes used are unstable and needed to add extra redox media. On the other hand, the obtained signal was not enhanced. Thus, it is still urgently needed to develop a robust, stable and high electric conductivity signal probe for
enhancing electrochemical signal to monitor telomerase activity.
Artificial enzymes (also known as enzyme mimics) with intrinsic enzymatic activities, adjustable size and nanostructures have garnered considerable attention. Compared with the natural enzymes, artificial enzymes possess many advantages such as low cost of synthesis, high stability and adjustable enzymatic activity, etc. Up to now, lots of nanomaterial-based artificial enzymes such as graphene oxide (Uzunoglu et al., 2016; Nafiey et al., 2016), Au (Zheng et al., 2011; Wen et al., 2016), Cu (Gawande et al., 2016), or Pt (Sun et al., 2015; Zhang et al., 2019) nanoparticles, magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles (Lu et al., 2017; Gao et al., 2017), and \(\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (Singh et al., 2017) have been widely used in biosensor and disease treatment. However, owing to most nanomaterials with easily aggregating and hardly modifying, some artificial enzymes were restricted their extensive application. Moreover, some artificial enzymes were just as mimetic catalyst in electrochemical biosensor, and needed to add redox mediators. So, they are hardly applied to amplify signal. Therefore, development of sensitive and multifunction nanozyme is required.In recent years, Metal-Organic frameworks (MOFs) with outstanding physical and chemical properties, including high surface area and porosity, suitable size and good biocompatibility (Zhu et al., 2014; Furukawa et al., 2013; Lee et al., 2009) have attracted tremendous attention. Until now, due to these features, MOFs and various modified MOFs have been widely used in lots of applications such as sensing (Lustig et al., 2017; Xu et al., 2016), catalysis (Huang et al., 2017), biomedical application (Min et al., 2019; Ren et al., 2019; Wang et al., 2019) and other areas (Wang et al., 2018; Yuan et al., 2018), especially in electrochemical sensor or electrocatalysis (Zheng et al., 2016; Kahn et al., 2017; Hu et al., 2017; Zhu et al., 2018; Wang et al., 2017; Gassensmith et al., 2014). However, MOFs in the above methods always acts the nanocarrier and the instinct property has not been employed. Thus, it is necessary to exploit the intrinsic activity of MOFs as biomimetic catalysis for
biosensor.
In the present study, we present a simple and sensitive strategy for telomeres activity detection based on Pt NPs decorated on the surface of MOFs as signal nanoprobe, Exo III as recycling amplification and the structure switch of triple-helix DNA as signal transduction (Scheme 1). The MOFs (P-MOF(Fe)) was prepared with metalloporphyrin (FeTCPP) as linker and Zr as node, then modified with Pt NPs to form Pt@P-MOF(Fe). In this system, P-MOF(Fe) was not only as nanocarrier but also as signal media. Pt NPs grown on the surface of P-MOF(Fe) could be hindered to aggregate, leading to Pt NPs possessed high stability. After modifying Pt@P-MOF(Fe) on the electrode, the reduction of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) was catalyzed by Pt NPs (Polaky et al., 2006) and then facilitated the formation of ferryl species \((\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{IV})=\mathrm{O})\) and a porphyrin radical cation (Rodriguez-López et al., 2001), generating the enhanced electrochemical signal. For the biosensor, as shown in Scheme 1(B), first, the telomerase primer (TP) in duplex (TP-aDNA1) could extend in the presence of dNTPs and telomerase. Next, the assistant DNA2 (aDNA2) in triple-helix DNA on the electrode could hybridize with the extension part of TP to trigger the Exo III cleavage process, following the extended TP-aDNA1 recycling, at the same time, to unfold the hairpin DNA (HpDNA) on the electrode. Then, after the cyclic process, numerous unfolded HpDNA would be released to further hybridize with capture DNA (cDNA), and a great deal of cDNA-conjugated nanomaterials (cDNA@Pt@P-MOF(Fe)) were successfully modified on the electrode, leading to an enhanced current signal for readout. Due to the recycling amplification of Exonuclease III and the synergy between P-MOF(Fe) and Pt NPs, the biosensor shows good sensitivity with detection limit of 20 cell \(\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\), and could be used in complex samples. The combination of nanozyme and MOFs provides a promising strategy for signal amplification and detecting biomolecules with enhanced sensitivity in biological application.
## 2. Materials and methods
2.1. Materials and reagents
Zirconium  Zirconium  oxychloride  octahydrate \(\mathrm{(ZrOCl_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O)}\) ,  benzoic  acid,  sodium borohydride (NaBH4), o-phenylenediamine (OPD), 1-(3-(dimethylamino)propyl)-3-ethylcarbodiimide  hydrochloride  (EDC, \(\geqslant\) \(98\%\) ), N-hydroxysuccinimide  (NHS),  and  hydrogen  peroxide \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)\) were  obtained  from Sigma-Aldrich (Shanghai, China). Iron(III)meso-5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin  chloride  (FeTCPP)  was purchased from J&K Scientific Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Carboxylic graphene oxide (CGO) was bought form Nanjing XFNano Materials Tech Co. Ltd. (Nanjing, China). Exonuclease III (Exo III) was purchased from Thermo Fisher Scientific (U.S.A). Hela cells were provided by Wannan Medical College. \(10\,\mathrm{\mM}\) phosphate buffer containing \(10\,\mathrm{\mM}\) KCl was used as oligonucleotide stock solution and hybridization solution. The DNA hybridization was carried out in \(20\;\mathrm{mM}\) PBS containing 20 mM KCl. All buffers were prepared with Millipore Milli-Q water \((\geqslant18.2\;\;\mathrm{M}\Omega\;\;\mathrm{cm})\) . The deoxynucleotide solution mixture (dNTPs) and all DNA oligonucleotides were from Sangon Inc. (Shanghai, China) and the sequences are given below: Hairpin DNA 1 (HpDNA1): \(5^{\prime}\) -NH2-AAAAAAAAATGGGAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAGGGTGTGT-3′ Hairpin DNA 2 (HpDNA2): 5′ -NH2-AAAAAAAAATGGGATAAAAAAAAAAAAAAATAGGGTGTGT-3′ Hairpin DNA 3 (HpDNA3): \(5^{\prime}\) -NH2-AAAAAAAAATGGGATTAAAAAAAAAAAAAATTAGGGTGTGT-3′ Hairpin DNA 4 (HpDNA4): \(5^{\prime}\) -NH2-AAAAAAAAATGGGATTGAAAAAAAAAAAAA GTTAGGGTGTGT-3′ Assistant DNA 1 (aDNA1): \(5^{\prime}\) -TGCTCGACGGATTCGATCT-3′
Assistant DNA 2 (aDNA2): 5'-ACCCTAACCTA-3'  Capture DNA (cDNA): 5'-SH-TTTTTTTTTAGGGTACCACCACCACACACCCT-3'  Telomerase primer (TP): 5'-AATCCGTCGAGCAGAGTT-3'
2.2. Apparatus
CHI 760D electrochemical workstation (Shanghai CH Instruments, China) with three electrode system, which are composed of Ag/AgCl electrode as the reference electrode, platinum wire as the counter electrode, and glassy carbon electrode (GCE) as the working electrode, was used for testing the cyclic voltammetry (CV) and linear sweep voltammetry (LSV). The UV–vis spectroscopy was determined on on Hitachi U-3900 UV/vis spectrometer (Hitachi, Japan). Transmission electron microscope images were performed on HT-7700 (Hitachi, Japan). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) were recorded using an S-4800 scanning electron microscope (Hitachi, Japan). X-ray di- \(\boxed{\begin{array}{r l}\end{array}}\) raction (XRD) were performed on shimadzu XRD-6000 (Shimadzu Co. Japan). PHI 5000 Versa Probe (UIVAC-PHI Co., Japan.) was used to analyze X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). Circular dichroism (CD) spectra were conducted on a chirascan circular dichroism chromatograph (Applied Photophysics LTD, England). The \(10\%\) native polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (PAGE) was run with electrophoresis apparatus (DYCP-31DN, Beijing Liuyi Biotechnology Co., Ltd) and scanned with FUSION FX7 EDGE (VILBER LOURMAT, France).
2.3. Synthesis of P-MOF(Fe)
The synthesis of P-MOF(Fe) was according to reported method previously with minor modifications (Feng et al., 2014). Typically, 6.0 mg FeTCPP, 90 mg \(\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2} \cdot 8 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\) and 840 mg benzoic acid were dissolved in 50 mL DMF and sonicated for 10 min, then the mixture was heated under \(80\,^{\circ} \mathrm{C}\) for \(5 \mathrm{~h}\). After cooling to room temperature, the resulting was collected by centrifuging and washed with DMF and ethanol. Finally, the product was re-dispersed in
1 ethanol.
2 2.4. Preparation of Pt@P-MOF(Fe)
3 The Pt@P-MOF(Fe) was prepared according to previous report (Zhang et al., 2018). First, 4 \(200~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(10\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}\) and \(50~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(4~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}\) P-MOF(Fe) were stirred with \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) 5 for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\) . After that, \(1\ \mathrm{mL}\) \((2~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}})\) ) \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) was added under stirring for another \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) . 6 Then, the mixture was washed with water for three times to obtain Pt@P-MOF(Fe) and 7 re-dispersed in water for further use.
8 2.5. Preparation of cDNA@Pt@P-MOF(Fe)
9 Initially, \(100~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(10~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) cDNA was added into \(50~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) Pt@P-MOF(Fe), and stirred \(20~\mathrm{h}\) 10 under \(4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Then, the mixture was washed with PBS for three times. The obtained 11 cDNA@Pt@P-MOF(Fe) was dispersed in PBS for further use.
12 2.6. Cell Culture and Telomerase Extraction
13 HeLa cells were cultured in Dulbecco's minima essential (DMEM) and maintained at 14 \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) in a humidified atmosphere ( \(95\%\) air and \(5\%\;\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) ).
15 The telomerase extraction process was performed as followed: about \(10^{7}\) cells which were 16 counted using a Beckman cell counter and transferred into a \(1.5~\mathrm{mL}\) EP tube. After washing 17 with PBS ( \(10\;\mathrm{mM})\) for two times, \(200~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) CHAPS lysis bu \(\boxed{\begin{array}{r l}\end{array}}\) er ( \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\) Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, 1.0 18 mM \(\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}\) , \(1.0\ \mathrm{mM}\) EGTA, \(0.1\,\mathrm{\mM}\) PMSF, \(5~\mathrm{mM}\) mercapto ethanol, \(0.5\%\) CHAPS, \(10\%\) 19 glycerol) was added into the tube and kept in an ice bath for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) . The obtained lysate was 20 centrifuged at 12 000 rpm for \(20\,\mathrm{\min}\) . Finally, the resulting extract was used or stored at 21 \(-80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . For comparison, prior to detection, the telomerase extract was also heat-treated at 22 \(95\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) .
23 2.7. Fabrication of biosensor and electrochemical detection
First, TP (15 \(\upmu\)M, 10 \(\upmu\)L) and aDNA1 (15 \(\upmu\)M, 10 \(\upmu\)L) were hybridized for 2 h under 37 \({}^{\circ}\)C. Then, 10 \(\upmu\)L telomerase extracts or standard telomerase and 10 \(\upmu\)L dNTPs (2.5 mM) in TRAP buffer (20 mM Tris–HCl, pH 8.3, 1.5 mM MgCl\(_2\), 63 mM KCl, 0.005% Tween 20, 1.0 mM EGTA, BSA 0.1 mg mL\(^{-1}\)) were added into the TP-aDNA1 duplex. After incubating under 37 \({}^{\circ}\)C for 3 h, the mixture was heated at 95 \({}^{\circ}\)C for 10 min and then stored at -20 \({}^{\circ}\)C.Before modifying the electrode, the glassy carbon electrode (GCE) was polished with \(\mathrm{Al}_2\mathrm{O}_3\) powder and washed with ultrapure water. After drying under room temperature, GCE was coated with 5 \(\upmu\)L of CGO aqueous solution (1.0 mg mL\(^{-1}\)), followed by drying in air. Then, 10 \(\upmu\)L of 400 mM EDC and 100 mM NHS were dropped on the surface of the electrode for 30 min and washed with ultrapure water. Subsequently, the electrode was incubated with 10 \(\upmu\)L of 2.5 \(\upmu\)M HpDNA for 4 h at 4 \({}^{\circ}\)C, followed aDNA2 (5 \(\upmu\)M, 10 \(\upmu\)L) was drop-casted on the surface of the electrode for 1.5 h at 37 \({}^{\circ}\)C to form triple-helix due to the two arm parts of HpDNA could hybridize with aDNA2. After washing, the triple-helix modified electrode was exposed to the mixture of TP-aDNA1 duplex and Exo III for 1.5 h under 37 \({}^{\circ}\)C. Finally, 10 \(\upmu\)L of cDNA@Pt@P-MOF(Fe) was dropped onto the electrode and incubated for 1.5 h. Then the biosensor was tested with optimized condition in 0.1 M PBS containing 10 mM \(\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}_2\).
## 3. Results and discussion
3.1. Characterization of P-MOF(Fe) and Pt@P-MOF(Fe)
In order to get P-MOF(Fe), the \(\mathrm{ZrOCl}_2 \cdot 8\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}\) as node and FeTCPP as linker were dispersed in DMF and heated at 80 \({}^{\circ}\)C for 5 h. After obtaining P-MOF(Fe), \(\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{PtCl}_6\) solution was mixed with P-MOF(Fe) and fresh \(\mathrm{NaBH}_4\) under stirring at room temperature to produce Pt@P-MOF(Fe) (scheme 1A). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission
electron microscopy (TEM) were performed to determine the morphology of Pt@P-MOF(Fe) P-MOF(Fe) and the distribution of Pt NPs (Fig. 1 and S1). From Fig. 1A and B, the obtained P-MOF(Fe) exhibited the ellipsoidal morphology with a uniform size of \(500\ \mathrm{nm}\). For comparison, after in situ growth of Pt NPs on the P-MOF(Fe), the morphology of P-MOF(Fe) was not changed (Fig. 1C and D). Moreover, the high-resolution TEM (Fig. 1D) showed Pt NPs was ca. \(2\ \mathrm{nm}\) in diameter, and dispersed uniformly. From the result of SEM elemental mapping indicated in Fig. S1, it can be seen that Pt element was appeared in Pt@P-MOF(Fe), which was consistent with TEM analysis. These results demonstrated Pt NPs formed on the surface of P-MOF(Fe) successfully.
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of P-MOF(Fe) and Pt@P-MOF(Fe) showed P-MOF(Fe) and Pt@P-MOF(Fe) exhibited three peaks between \(4^{\circ}-10^{\circ}\), which are \(4.80^{\circ}\), \(7.08^{\circ}\) and \(9.67^{\circ}\) and index to the (-220), (410) and (-150), respectively (Fig. 2), suggesting the P-MOF(Fe) assemble successfully (Feng et al., 2014). As shown in Fig. 2, curve b, the peaks of Pt NPs were appeared at \(40{-}80^{\circ}\) (JCPDS: 65-2868), indicating the Pt NPs were formed, which were the parts of the products and did not affect the structure of P-MOF(Fe).
3.2. Electrocatalytic behavior of Pt@P-MOF(Fe)
The electrocatalytic activity of Pt@P-MOF(Fe) was first investigated in \(0.1\ \mathrm{M}\) PBS. As depicted in Fig. S2, compared with bare GCE (curve a), the P-MOF(Fe)/GCE showed clearly reduction current, indicating P-MOF(Fe) could electro-catalyze towards \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) reduction (curve b). The electrocatalytic activity of P-MOF(Fe) was from the linker FeTCPP which is a kind of metalloporphyrin, and used as mimetic enzyme in biosensor. Due to the structure of P-MOF(Fe), FeTCPP was avoided forming \(\mu\)-oxide dimers, leading to improve the catalytic activity. When Pt@P-MOF(Fe) was modified on GCE (curve c), the current response of Pt@P-MOF(Fe)/GCE was higher than that of P-MOF(Fe)/GCE, owing to Pt NPs distributed
uniformly on the surface of P-MOF(Fe) and exhibited excellent catalytic activity towards to the reduction of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) (Polsky, et al., 2006). These results proved the excellent electrocatalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{P-MOF}(\mathrm{Fe})\) to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\).3.3. Feasibility of electrochemical DNA biosensorBased on the electrocatalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{P-MOF}(\mathrm{Fe})\), the electrochemical telomeres biosensor was constructed. First, the feasibility of the sensor was investigated. As shown in the result of circular dichroism (CD) (Fig. S3), when HpDNA and aDNA2 are mixed, a negative band at \(\sim210\;\mathrm{nm}\) was appeared, confirming the formation of the triplex (Antony et al., 1999; Gowers et al., 1999; Manzini et al., 1990). In order to verify cDNA modified on the surface of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{P-MOF}(\mathrm{Fe})\), UV-vis absorption spectrometry of the cDNA@Pt@P-MOF(Fe) was tested and shown in Fig. 3A. Compared with P-MOF(Fe) (curve b), cDNA@Pt@P-MOF(Fe) had a peak at \(260\ \mathrm{nm}\) (curve c), which is the character peak of cDNA (curve a), indicating the cDNA was modified on the surface of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{P-MOF}(\mathrm{Fe})\). Cyclic voltammetry (CV) was also conducted to understand the feasibility of the sensor and the results were shown in Fig. 3B. Compared with the solution of TP + aDNA1 without telomerase extract (curve a), the sensor showed an increased current in the presence of telomerase extract (curve b), which implied that the TP could extend and then hybridized with aDNA2, thus the HpDNA could be released to hybridize with cDNA, leading to \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{P-MOF}(\mathrm{Fe})\) bound on the surface of GCE for electrocatalytic of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Upon the addition of Exo III, the reduction current increased (curve c), which should be contributed to the Exo III cleavage process and the extended TP-aDNA1 recycling. So, more cDNA@Pt@P-MOF(Fe) could be introduced to the surface of GCE, inducing a significantly amplified current signal. The Gel electrophoresis was used to further confirm the feasibility of the strategy. From the Fig. S4, aDNA1 (lane 1), aDNA2 (lane 2), cDNA (lane 3), TP (lane 4) and HpDNA (lane 5) had the single migration band. When HpDNA was incubated with aDNA2 (lane 6), there was
a new low migration rate on the gel, suggesting HpDNA was hybridized with aDNA2 to form HpDNA-aDNA2 triple-helix. After incubating HpDNA-aDNA2 triple-helix with Exo III (lane 7), the migration rate of the band was the same to lane 6, indicating Exo III did not cleave the triple-helix. When HpDNA was hybridized with cDNA, the band with lower migration speed appeared on the gel (lane 8), revealing HpDNA-cDNA duplex was formed. Compared with TP-aDNA1 duplex (lane 9), the TP-aDNA1 duplex incubated with the dNTPs and telomerase extract could form a new band with lower migration (lane 10), which showed that the TP was extended. When the TP-aDNA1 duplex incubated with Exo III (lane 11), the mixture of TP-aDNA1, dNTPs and telomerase extract incubated with Exo III (lane 12) and HpDNA-cDNA duplex incubated with Exo III (lane 13), the new bands on the gel were corresponded to lane 9, lane 10 and lane 8, respectively, demonstrating Exo III could not cleave them. After incubating aDNA2 with the mixture of TP-aDNA1 duplex, dNTPs and telomerase extract, there was a new band observed on the gel (lane 14), which is lower than lane 10, suggesting the extension of TP-aDNA1 duplex could hybridize with aDNA2. All of the above results reflected that the designed method was feasible.
3.4. Optimization of detection conditions
To maximize the electrochemical signal, we investigated the experiment conditions, including the concentration of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), the arm length of HpDNA, the cleavage temperature and time of Exo III, the concentration of Exo III, the incubation time of telomerase extension and the hybrid time of HpDNA with cDNA. First, the effects of the concentration of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) was studied and the result was shown in Fig. S5A. The peak current increased with the concentration of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) increasing and became stable at \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\), indicating the electrocatalytic e- ciency reached maximize with \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). So, the optimal concentration of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\). To investigate the effect of the arm length of HpDNA, the biosensor was detected with different length of arm in HpDNA from 5 bases
to 8 bases (from HpDNA1 to HpDNA4, arm length of HpDNA with 5 bases (i.e. arm length of HpDNA1), arm length of HpDNA with 6 bases (i.e. arm length of HpDNA2), arm length of HpDNA with 7 bases (i.e. arm length of HpDNA3), and arm length of HpDNA with 8 bases (i.e. arm length of HpDNA4)). As shown in Fig. 4A, with the arm length of HpDNA increased, the formed HpDNA-aDNA2 was more stable. When the arm length of HpDNA with 8 bases (HpDNA4), the formed triple-helix could not release aDNA2 to hybridize with the extension of TP, resulting the HpDNA was hard to hybridize with cDNA. When the arm length of HpDNA with 5 bases (HpDNA1), the triple-helix is unstable, the current changed slightly in the absence or presence of telomerase extract. When the arm length of HpDNA with 7 bases (HpDNA3), the improved current signal is similar to that with 6 bases (HpDNA2) in the presence of telomerase extract. Due to the HpDNA with 7 bases had lower current signal than that with 6 bases in the absence of telomerase extract, thus, 7 bases were selected as the optimal arm length of HpDNA.The cleavage temperature and time of Exo III were also studied. As depicted in Fig. 4B and C, the current increased with increasing the cleavage temperature and time, and then reached the maximum at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(1.5\;\mathrm{h}\). Thus, \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(1.5\;\mathrm{h}\) were selected for the best cleavage temperature and cleavage time, respectively. The effect of Exo III concentration was shown in Fig. S5B. With the increase of Exo III concentration, the current increased and reached maximum at \(2.5\;\mathrm{U/\upmu L}\). Therefore, Exo III concentration with \(2.5\;\mathrm{U/\upmu L}\) was chosen as the optimal condition for the throughout experiment. When the TP-aDNA1 was incubated with telomerase extract more than \(2.5\;\mathrm{h}\), the current approached a platform (Fig. 4D), indicating the reaction may complete in \(2.5\;\mathrm{h}\). So, the optimal incubation time for telomerase extract is \(2.5\;\mathrm{h}\). The incubation time between HpDNA and cDNA was also the key role in the whole experiment. The result in Fig. S5C showed the current signal reached the maximum value with the incubation time for \(1.5\;\mathrm{h}\). Then, the optimal incubation time
for HpDNA hybridization with cDNA was selected \(1.5\,\mathrm{h}\).
3.5. Detection of Telomerase Activity
The Hela cells were used as the model for the experiment under the optimized condition. In Fig. 5A, it could be seen that the LSV current response increased with the increasing HeLa cells concentration, due to more HpDNA released, leading to more HpDNA-cDNA form and more Pt@P-MOF(Fe) could bind on the surface of electrode. In addition, the peak current had a good proportion to the logarithm of the HeLa cell concentration from 50 to \(5\!\times\!10^{5}\) HeLa cells \(\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) and the detection limit was 20 cells, which was lower than that of electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (1000 Hela cell \(\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\)) (Yang et al., 2011), electrochemiluminescence amplification assay (1000 Hela cell \(\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\)) (Zhou et al., 2009) and so on (Table S1). These results implied that the synergy between P-MOF(Fe) and Pt NPs could improve the electrochemical signal and develop the sensitivity of the biosensor.
3.6. Application in human serum sample
To further evaluate the practical application of the biosensor, the serum samples from the patients was tested (Fig. S6). Compared with the current response obtained in PBS, the current response collected in human serum was slightly lower, suggesting the biosensor with good sensitivity in determination of telomerase activity in real sample. In order to explore the stability of the biosensor, the biosensor was stored at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for ten days and tested each day (Fig. S7). The result shows that the current signal still maintained \(95\%\) in comparing with the original current signal, revealing the biosensor with satisfactory stability.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, an electrochemical telomerase activity sensor with Pt@P-MOF(Fe) as signal probe, allosteric switch of DNA and Exo III recycling amplification was successfully fabricated. The P-MOF(Fe) with large surface area not only acted as the carrier but also acted as signal media. Pt NPs in situ grown on the surface of the P-MOF(Fe) demonstrated excellent catalytic activity to the reduction of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Due to the synergy between P-MOF(Fe) and Pt NPs, the electrochemical signal could be enhanced. Furthermore, the allosteric switch of DNA and the aid of Exo III could induce more HpDNA release and introduce more Pt@P-MOF(Fe) to the surface of the electrode, leading the amplified electrocatalytic current readout. Given the recycling amplification of Exo III and the synergy between Pt NPs and P-MOF(Fe), the method could have good sensitivity and could be used in complex matrixes. This artificial nanozyme system not only opens a new way to exploit the intrinsic activity of MOFs and employ it as signal media to amplify the signal but also offers a novel strategy to apply the artificial nanozyme in electrochemical biosensor. Although the sensor remains a challenging for detecting very small amount of analyte, we believe that the combination of MOF and electrochemical sensor have a great promise for the analyte detection. The prospective future is to explore the properties of MOF to detect the analyte in vivo.
